1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:20,395
The general gave me a car with no doors and told me to pursue justice.
This nigga had seen the Lamborghini. This nigga knew what I drove.
Nigga still rolled out a rich man's shopping cart and looked at me
like a favor had occurred. I looked at the car, then at the nigga,
then back at the car.

2
00:00:20,645 --> 00:00:42,403
Somewhere a manufacturer had built this motherfucker without imagining
the nigga who would be insulted by it. I looked at the car. I looked
at him. "Nigga, justice could at least have windows." It was a low
white racing machine with a roll cage, enormous tires, and the
temperament of a lawn mower that had inherited money.

3
00:00:42,653 --> 00:01:07,603
The other competitors stood beside identical cars in matching
uniforms. Twenty-four captains from twenty-four fictional police
forces, all invited to show the world what a real officer could do.
Saint had put me at the far end in a uniform two sizes too small. I
came in my own. Chief Knox walked the line behind me with the
ceremonial sword under one arm.

4
00:01:07,853 --> 00:01:30,807
She had taken off her summer jacket because the sun was hot and she
intended to insult several men comfortably. "Your entry conditions
permit a personal vehicle," she told Saint. "Only if it meets the
spirit of the contest." My Lamborghini rolled through the gate behind
her with Mercedes driving and Porsche leaning over the passenger door
eating a peach.

5
00:01:31,057 --> 00:01:53,436
The entire line of police captains turned. "That's the nigga's police
car?" "That's the nigga's slow police car," Tank said. "Nigga has a
faster one?" "Nigga has an island full of faster ones." "How do I join
this nigga's department?" Knox looked down the line. "Start by
standing up straight." Twenty-three niggas found their spines
together.

6
00:01:53,686 --> 00:02:17,105
One man took off his sunglasses and put them back on as if the first
look had been a problem with the lenses. I pointed at the dark blue
hood. "That nigga got spirit." Saint examined the gold police shield
on the door. His reflection looked miserable in my paint. "A single
passenger is permitted," he said. Mercedes handed me the keys. "Good.

7
00:02:17,355 --> 00:02:39,577
I'm coming." Shawna stood beneath the awning with Rosa. She wore
yellow today, and every breeze seemed to know exactly what to do with
the skirt. I went over to kiss her before the start. "What do you want
if I win?" I asked. "A blue piano." "Done." "On a boat." "Bigger
done." "That stays level when the sea moves." I looked at Tank.

8
00:02:39,827 --> 00:03:02,963
"Nigga, find an engineer." He held up his phone without looking away
from Rosa. "Already searching." My wife brushed a little dust from my
shoulder. "And you back in one piece." "You asked for the expensive
things last." The pursuit course wound from the stadium up through the
hillside, across a dry riverbed, through the old port, and back along
the sea wall.

9
00:03:03,213 --> 00:03:27,435
Three gates along the way held red flags. A driver had to bring back
one from each gate. Opponents could take a flag by touching the long
padded arm fixed to the side of their car against another car's red
target. That was the official game. The unofficial game was twenty-
three niggas looking at me like I had personally insulted every road
they'd ever driven.

10
00:03:27,685 --> 00:03:51,177
Captain Graves came over while Mercedes fastened her belt. "You
understand there are no second chances on the upper road," he said.
"Then don't waste your first one staring at my car." His eyes flicked
toward the dashboard. Toward the seat. Toward the wheel. "It's a very
good car." "You can say beautiful, nigga. It won't make you less
police." He almost smiled.

11
00:03:51,427 --> 00:04:09,202
At the starting line, Mercedes adjusted the rearview mirror toward her
face. "Need that," I said. "I need to know how I look when we win."
"You look rich." "Then stop talking and make me accurate." The gun
sounded. Twenty-four engines hit the morning together.

12
00:04:09,453 --> 00:04:32,647
We came off the line in a cloud of white dust, the little pursuit cars
jumping over the rough ground like angry goats. I let three pass me
before the first bend. Mercedes watched the mirror. "Graves on your
right. Bald nigga on your left. Handsome one trying to get between
them." "They all handsome if they behind us." I took the inside turn.

13
00:04:32,897 --> 00:04:53,535
The padded arm on the nearest car swung toward my door. I moved just
enough to let him miss and watched his arm strike the car beside him
instead. Both men shouted. A red flag shot up from the struck car's
rear basket. Mercedes caught it before it touched the ground. She held
it across her lap.

14
00:04:53,785 --> 00:05:15,662
"Souvenir." The first gate stood at the bottom of the dry riverbed.
Drivers funneled toward it, tires fighting loose stone. Graves went
high along the bank. Two others stayed low. I saw the gatekeeper lift
three flags for them and calculate how long it would take to get
another one ready for me. I didn't give him the time.

15
00:05:15,913 --> 00:05:35,827
The Lamborghini hit the bank, rose across it, and came down beside the
gate like a dark blue argument. Mercedes leaned out and took the flag
from his raised hand with two fingers. "Thank you, sweetheart." The
nigga bowed. We were already gone. Mercedes looked back at the
gatekeeper.

16
00:05:36,077 --> 00:05:59,737
"Nigga bowed." "Polite nigga." "Other niggas just got a flag." "Other
niggas didn't bring you." She settled deeper into the seat. "Nigga,
that's the kind of navigation I enjoy." I grinned. Behind us the
gatekeeper was still waving, some nigga taking pictures of a nigga
taking a good morning personally. On the upper road the sea opened
beneath us.

17
00:05:59,987 --> 00:06:20,413
It was the kind of blue that made a man forgive weather for everything
else it had ever done. White buildings clung to the hillside. People
watched from balconies holding breakfast plates, cheering whenever my
siren cut through the engines. A woman threw flowers. Mercedes caught
one and tucked it behind her ear.

18
00:06:20,663 --> 00:06:41,978
"These people understand arrival." I was smiling when the black truck
came around the blind curve. It didn't belong in the race. It had no
number, no padded arm, and six men standing in the open back holding
rifles. The first shot knocked a piece off the stone wall beside my
head. Mercedes dropped the flower.

19
00:06:42,228 --> 00:07:05,315
"Nigga, that's real." "I noticed the enthusiasm." I swung the
Lamborghini behind a pursuit car as its driver ducked. The truck
blocked most of the road. Beyond it the second gate fluttered red and
cheerful, not understanding its career had changed. Graves braked
beside me. His car struck the wall, sparks running down the side.

20
00:07:05,565 --> 00:07:29,489
He looked at the gunmen, then back toward the stadium below. His
general's flags flew over both. "Keep the other drivers back," I told
him. "I can engage—" "Nigga, keep them alive. That's an engagement." I
drove at the truck. Mercedes caught the handle above her door. She
didn't scream. I liked that about her. Plenty of people could act
brave in a safe room.

21
00:07:29,739 --> 00:07:48,145
Mercedes looked death in the face and got offended about the outfit.
"If we die," she said, "I want you to know this dashboard is too low
for my legs." "Write the factory when we win." The truck driver turned
to block the narrow gap by the wall. I let him believe I wanted it.

22
00:07:48,395 --> 00:08:09,775
At the last instant I took the raised stone verge on the other side,
put two tires high, and brought the Lamborghini onto its side wheels.
The horizon tipped. Mercedes pressed herself against the seat as the
sea moved above her shoulder. We passed between truck and cliff with
my door a hand's width from the rock.

23
00:08:10,025 --> 00:08:33,228
One gunman stared directly into my windshield as we went by. His mouth
opened. He had enough time to appreciate the work and not enough time
to improve his aim. The car dropped onto four wheels beyond the truck.
I turned it across the road and fired back through my open window. The
men scattered behind the truck's cab. One dropped his rifle.

24
00:08:33,478 --> 00:08:53,971
Another jumped into the ditch and discovered the ditch contained an
extremely angry goat. Mercedes watched him struggle. "Local police,"
she said. Behind the truck, Graves and two captains had left their
cars. Together they overwhelmed the driver while my shots kept the men
in the back from rising.

25
00:08:54,221 --> 00:09:14,784
The whole thing ended with five gunmen on the ground and the sixth
trying to negotiate with livestock. Graves came around the cab with
his jaw clenched. "Those are Saint's men." "Nigga, did the matching
outfits help?" He looked at me for a long moment. "I gave him my word
that I would compete fairly." "Then do.

26
00:09:15,034 --> 00:09:37,232
You didn't give him your fucking eyes." The other captains gathered
around us. The bald one had a cut above his eyebrow. The handsome one
was bleeding through his sleeve. Nobody wanted to get back in a car
until they knew whether the next corner had another truck waiting. I
understood. A nigga can agree to lose a race without agreeing to lose
a lung.

27
00:09:37,482 --> 00:09:57,806
These niggas had come with trophies in mind, not funerals. I looked at
the wounded nigga, the bald nigga, the handsome nigga no longer
worrying about his sleeve. All of them were waiting for the nigga
Saint had told them to beat. I took the radio from the truck and put
it on the hood. Saint's voice crackled out.

28
00:09:58,056 --> 00:10:21,560
"Has the captain stopped?" I pressed the button. "Just to admire the
goats." Silence. Then I heard a chair scrape somewhere. Mercedes
leaned close to the radio. "The girls say hello." I left it on the
hood. Graves looked down the mountain. The whole race had stopped
because I had stopped. Twenty-three proud men waited to see if I would
go on.

29
00:10:21,810 --> 00:10:42,755
"We finish," I said. The bald captain nodded first. "Behind you?" "If
you can keep up." Nigga grinned like I'd given him his birthday. The
second gatekeeper handed Mercedes a flag while bowing so low his cap
fell off. At the port we came into streets lined with hanging laundry
and cheering workers.

30
00:10:43,005 --> 00:11:06,170
Every other car stayed close behind us, a moving wall of captains who
had come to beat me and discovered a better use for their engines.
Then a delivery van rolled into the crossing. Its driver jumped out
shouting that the brakes were gone. The van rolled downhill toward an
outdoor café full of people who had turned their chairs to watch the
race.

31
00:11:06,420 --> 00:11:29,348
I didn't have any Blue Thunder in my hands yet. I had been saving it
because a nigga couldn't spend electricity like money just because he
happened to have some. Now I gave the engine a short bright pulse. The
Lamborghini leaped forward. I came alongside the van, matched its
awful rolling speed, and pressed my front quarter against it. Metal
folded.

32
00:11:29,598 --> 00:11:53,780
My beautiful paint cried without a voice. We turned together, slowly
enough to hurt, hard enough to shove my shoulders against the belt.
The van struck a stack of empty fish crates instead of the café.
Crates exploded across the street. One landed perfectly on the café
owner's head. He lifted it off, looked at the untouched tables, and
held up both thumbs.

33
00:11:54,030 --> 00:12:17,667
I rested my forehead against the wheel for one breath. Mercedes
touched my arm. "You there?" "Yeah." "Good. Because if you pass out
I'm driving, and this bitch already offended my knees." I laughed and
straightened. My left hand shook once. I kept it low and used the
right. At the third gate, the flags had disappeared. Saint hadn't been
subtle.

34
00:12:17,917 --> 00:12:36,816
He had simply ordered them removed while we were coming down the hill.
A little man in a cream suit stood beside the empty pole looking like
his stomach wanted to leave without him. "Regrettable," he said.
"Without the final pennant, no competitor can record a complete—"
Mercedes got out.

35
00:12:37,066 --> 00:12:58,956
She walked past him, opened the nearby shed, and found twenty-four red
flags on a chair. The man closed his eyes. "Nigga," she said softly,
"I have found married men in smaller hiding places." She handed one to
every captain. "Nigga, say thank you," she told the first. He said it.
So did the next nigga. And the next.

36
00:12:59,206 --> 00:13:17,012
By the fifth nigga she was smiling like a woman handing out report
cards to a class that had finally learned manners. "Good nigga.
Another good nigga. Look at y'all. International courtesy." The little
man in the cream suit wished he had gone into the shed with the flags.

37
00:13:17,262 --> 00:13:37,729
Then she got back in my car and crossed her legs as if she had stepped
out to powder her nose. We entered the stadium together. The crowd
rose. They had seen the truck on the live feed before somebody cut the
signal. They had seen the rescue at the café after Porsche somehow
found a different camera and put it back on.

38
00:13:37,979 --> 00:13:58,888
Now they saw twenty-four cars pouring through the arch, every police
captain on his feet behind the wheel except me because a nigga was
tired and had a roof. Graves tried to pass me on the final straight. I
respected him for it. I also beat him by half a car. At the line he
laughed openly for the first time.

39
00:13:59,138 --> 00:14:24,946
"Had to try." "Good captain." "Better driver," he admitted. "Nigga,
don't stop. I'm enjoying this." Saint waited beside the winners'
platform holding the first golden laurel. His smile looked like he'd
had it installed against medical advice. Before he could speak, the
bald captain took the microphone. "We agreed on something out there,"
he said. Twenty-two men stood behind him. Graves joined them.

40
00:14:25,196 --> 00:14:44,704
"Captain Aiden led us through a live attack, saved civilians during
the race, and still crossed first. We recognize him as the champion of
pursuit." The crowd roared. Saint had to put the laurel around my neck
himself. I bent just enough to make it possible and looked him in the
eyes while he did it.

41
00:14:44,954 --> 00:15:03,996
"Nigga," I said quietly, "your games got expensive fast." He released
the ribbon like it had burned him. Then the captains started lining up
for pictures. Not one. All of them. Some wanted me between them and
their cars. One wanted me to hold his radio and say his call sign.

42
00:15:04,246 --> 00:15:25,872
The bald captain admitted he'd told his department he would bring back
my autograph if he couldn't bring back the trophy. Knox stood off to
the side, watching twenty-three men wait their turn. "You collecting
departments now?" she asked. One nigga behind her asked whether I
could sign his cap. Another nigga had brought his badge.

43
00:15:26,122 --> 00:15:42,660
A third nigga wanted a photograph for his mother but admitted, when
Mercedes looked at him, that his mother did not know how to open
photographs on a phone. "Nigga, it's for you," she said. "It's for
me." "Let the nigga enjoy himself," I told her.

44
00:15:42,910 --> 00:15:59,042
"I take excellent pictures." "Chief, they followed me home." Shawna
came through the crowd and put her hands on my face. She looked at my
pupils, my hands, the little line of strain under my mouth. "One
burst," I said before she asked.

45
00:15:59,292 --> 00:16:19,587
"And one side of the car." "Baby, both sides still exist." She kissed
me, slow enough for every camera to understand that photographs had
limits. When she let me go, she looked over my shoulder. At the harbor
below, workers were lowering an enormous blue piano onto the deck of a
white yacht.

46
00:16:19,837 --> 00:16:44,059
Tank stood beside them, arms folded, while an engineer explained the
platform beneath it with wild hand movements. My wife blinked.
"Already?" "Nigga found an engineer." Her laugh hit me harder than
Darius had. I took the laurel off and put it around Mercedes's neck.
She looked down at it, suddenly quiet. "For your knees," I said. She
touched the gold leaves.

47
00:16:44,310 --> 00:16:58,784
"You didn't have to do that." "You caught the flags." Porsche leaned
against her shoulder for the picture. "And the niggas caught feelings.
Look at them." The captains were still standing in line. Saint was not
smiling anymore.
